WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.148 --> 00:00:02.696
- [Narrator] It would be
so hard to go back home

00:00:02.696 --> 00:00:05.241
after everything happened and try

00:00:05.241 --> 00:00:06.842
to just live your life normally.

00:00:06.842 --> 00:00:09.163
I think that's partially why my family

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:11.961
just chose not to really talk about it.

00:00:11.961 --> 00:00:15.020
They just wanted to
leave it all in the past.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.853
(emotional music)

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.164
- During World War II
Manzanar was an American

00:00:24.164 --> 00:00:27.065
incarceration camp in which
Japanese Americans were

00:00:27.065 --> 00:00:31.232
held against their will in
the name of national security.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:40.255
- I want to go to Manzanar
because I don't think

00:00:40.255 --> 00:00:42.291
I know my own history that well.

00:00:42.291 --> 00:00:43.575
Both of my grandparents were held

00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:45.247
in camps when they were children.

00:00:45.247 --> 00:00:48.247
- My family didn't really
talk about it growing up.

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:51.582
Whenever I'd ask about
it like my grandparents

00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:54.224
were too young to remember
and they said that

00:00:54.224 --> 00:00:56.139
they never talked about
it with their parents.

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:59.639
- My family was actually not in the camps.

00:01:00.586 --> 00:01:02.741
It was more like just remember,
this is also something

00:01:02.741 --> 00:01:06.374
that happened here in
California which I am born

00:01:06.374 --> 00:01:07.640
and raised in California.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.983
- I want to come out of this you know

00:01:09.983 --> 00:01:13.840
more knowledgeable but also
I want to come out of this

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.994
with a greater understanding
of what happened.

00:01:15.994 --> 00:01:17.407
I want to understand what it felt like

00:01:17.407 --> 00:01:19.477
and understand what
they had to go through.

00:01:19.477 --> 00:01:20.680
- Something like the story of the camps,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.326
like visiting the camps, being there,

00:01:22.326 --> 00:01:24.088
talking to people about
it like amongst ourselves

00:01:24.088 --> 00:01:26.838
as Japanese Americans,
understanding our own story,

00:01:26.838 --> 00:01:28.541
is so key 'cause we're
not going to be able

00:01:28.541 --> 00:01:32.708
to move forward if we don't
know where we came from.

00:01:44.876 --> 00:01:47.778
- When the Japanese attacked Pearl Harbor,

00:01:47.778 --> 00:01:51.182
our west coast became a
potential combat zone.

00:01:51.182 --> 00:01:53.608
Living in that zone are
more than 100,000 persons

00:01:53.608 --> 00:01:55.440
of Japanese ancestry.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.114
Military authorities therefore determined

00:01:57.114 --> 00:01:59.364
that all of them, citizens and aliens

00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:01.027
alike would have to move.

00:02:01.027 --> 00:02:04.173
The evacuees cooperated wholeheartedly.

00:02:04.173 --> 00:02:06.040
The many loyal among
them felt that this was

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.492
a sacrifice they could make

00:02:07.492 --> 00:02:10.325
in behalf of America's war effort.

00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:14.426
(gentle guitar music)

00:02:18.126 --> 00:02:21.375
- The air felt clean, but also like

00:02:21.375 --> 00:02:22.208
you're in the middle of nowhere.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.791
Like almost like placelessness.

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:28.023
- It's very hot.
- It's a little hot.

00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:30.273
All things considered here.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.114
- This amazing amazing
ranger comes to meet us,

00:02:33.114 --> 00:02:36.096
and she ushers us over to the barracks.

00:02:36.096 --> 00:02:38.422
- A lot of Manzanar is gone, so most of

00:02:38.422 --> 00:02:39.902
the original buildings are gone.

00:02:39.902 --> 00:02:41.812
Manzanar wouldn't even
be here for us to visit

00:02:41.812 --> 00:02:44.219
if it weren't for the
efforts of Japanese Americans

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:45.505
who made the pilgrimage back to the site

00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:47.631
and then who successfully
lobbied the government

00:02:47.631 --> 00:02:48.464
to preserve it.

00:02:48.464 --> 00:02:51.783
- These barracks have been
rebuilt in the same spot

00:02:51.783 --> 00:02:54.781
that the ones were during World War II.

00:02:54.781 --> 00:02:57.087
The barracks here were
100 feet by 20 feet.

00:02:57.087 --> 00:02:58.587
When you got here.

00:03:14.616 --> 00:03:17.624
- Their immediate reaction
is this is too nice.

00:03:17.624 --> 00:03:19.161
- There's just like bunch of Japanese

00:03:19.161 --> 00:03:21.298
slash Japanese Americans that were

00:03:21.298 --> 00:03:24.048
squished into a very small space.

00:03:34.483 --> 00:03:36.545
I can't imagine.

00:03:36.545 --> 00:03:37.530
- Not comfortable.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.252
When they came here,
how long were they told

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:41.752
they were going to be staying?

00:03:44.215 --> 00:03:45.420
- That must be tough to not be able

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.725
to tell your kids how long
they have to expect to stay.

00:03:47.725 --> 00:03:49.609
- I just kept thinking
about the first night.

00:03:49.609 --> 00:03:51.642
Imagine the first night
walking into this barrack.

00:03:51.642 --> 00:03:54.267
Imagine the first night with your suitcase

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.957
just like unpacked at
the foot of your bed,

00:03:55.957 --> 00:03:57.640
unsure how long you're gonna be there.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.817
- We went outside, and
while we were on the grounds

00:03:59.817 --> 00:04:02.741
we did notice that there
were like basketball courts.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:05.054
- This basketball court has been redone,

00:04:05.054 --> 00:04:07.637
but it is historically correct.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.263
- Seeing the basketball hoops there

00:04:12.263 --> 00:04:14.425
just proves that despite all the injustice

00:04:14.425 --> 00:04:16.393
and things that were
being placed upon them,

00:04:16.393 --> 00:04:17.521
they didn't let it break their spirit.

00:04:17.521 --> 00:04:21.625
Ball was still life, which
is like super dope to me.

00:04:21.625 --> 00:04:23.209
- We went into the visitor's center

00:04:23.209 --> 00:04:25.090
and that was really fascinating obviously

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.778
seeing like all the
propaganda from the time.

00:04:27.778 --> 00:04:29.437
- It was from a nationally
syndicated columnist

00:04:29.437 --> 00:04:31.568
who said "herd 'em up, pack 'em off

00:04:31.568 --> 00:04:33.646
"and give 'em the inside
room in the Badlands.

00:04:33.646 --> 00:04:36.315
"Let 'em be pinched, hurt,
hungry and dead up against it.

00:04:36.315 --> 00:04:37.679
"Personally I hate the Japanese,

00:04:37.679 --> 00:04:40.262
"and that goes for all of 'em."

00:04:51.472 --> 00:04:53.143
- The ranger took out these family records

00:04:53.143 --> 00:04:57.310
for those of us who had families
that were put in the camps.

00:04:58.282 --> 00:05:00.615
- [Woman] Oh that's so cool.

00:05:01.519 --> 00:05:05.324
- This list where the family's from,

00:05:05.324 --> 00:05:07.167
part of California, and this list

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:09.308
where they're heading to where after

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:10.385
they leave camp, where they're going.

00:05:10.385 --> 00:05:11.817
And if you look at, this is all my family.

00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:14.797
Chicago, Illinois, Chicago,
Illinois, Chicago Illinois.

00:05:14.797 --> 00:05:17.385
I mean they made it there
because that's where I'm from.

00:05:17.385 --> 00:05:20.461
- Seeing it on paper and
seeing like the actual record,

00:05:20.461 --> 00:05:21.922
like that's like a piece of my family's

00:05:21.922 --> 00:05:23.573
history right there you know?

00:05:23.573 --> 00:05:25.175
Like an artifact.

00:05:25.175 --> 00:05:28.560
- Sean, I found both your
grandparents block addresses

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.317
which if we want to try and go see them,

00:05:30.317 --> 00:05:32.910
I would probably have
better luck than you did.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:34.690
- [Woman] So block five is?

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:37.257
- Is the one where Paul was, yeah.

00:05:37.257 --> 00:05:41.424
- [Woman] And Paul is Sean,
Paul is your grandfather?

00:05:46.753 --> 00:05:49.586
(emotional music)

00:05:55.071 --> 00:05:58.321
This is where my grandpa's barrack was.

00:06:00.281 --> 00:06:01.114
So yeah.

00:06:04.068 --> 00:06:04.901
- [Woman] And now you're here.

00:06:04.901 --> 00:06:06.151
- Now I'm here.

00:06:12.377 --> 00:06:14.508
- They hadn't excavated the
site, so our park ranger

00:06:14.508 --> 00:06:17.269
told us that the plate had probably been

00:06:17.269 --> 00:06:20.384
at that barrack site for over 70 years.

00:06:20.384 --> 00:06:21.276
- We're gonna put it back.

00:06:21.276 --> 00:06:22.693
- [Patricia] Yes.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:27.519
The morning before we went to Manzanar

00:06:27.519 --> 00:06:30.823
was the first time that my
grandfather forgot my name,

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.632
and so to be at Manzanar the next day

00:06:32.632 --> 00:06:34.566
and to see his barrack, I think for me

00:06:34.566 --> 00:06:36.522
it was like survivors of the camps

00:06:36.522 --> 00:06:38.013
can't be the only ones telling the story

00:06:38.013 --> 00:06:40.094
because they're fading,
but for us to make sure

00:06:40.094 --> 00:06:42.031
that the lessons continue, we have to keep

00:06:42.031 --> 00:06:45.612
going back to the camps whether
literally or figuratively,

00:06:45.612 --> 00:06:47.986
'cause there's so much to learn.

00:06:47.986 --> 00:06:49.486
- It looks like...

00:06:52.858 --> 00:06:54.959
- This is definitely, I told
you it was a sewer pipe!

00:06:54.959 --> 00:06:56.805
- 'Cause the diameter's
too big, so water coming in

00:06:56.805 --> 00:06:59.204
has to be in pressure, so
you get those smaller lines.

00:06:59.204 --> 00:07:02.364
- I am probably holding a sewage pipe.

00:07:02.364 --> 00:07:05.531
(gentle guitar music)

00:07:14.187 --> 00:07:16.812
- We went to the cemetery,
and that was just

00:07:16.812 --> 00:07:18.867
very like peaceful and serene.

00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:21.778
- It's just covered in these
like colorful origami cranes,

00:07:21.778 --> 00:07:24.216
and there is this like windstorm blowing

00:07:24.216 --> 00:07:27.061
and it was like blowing the cranes.

00:07:27.061 --> 00:07:28.438
- The whole thing in general was actually

00:07:28.438 --> 00:07:30.621
a lot more emotional for me
than I thought it would be.

00:07:30.621 --> 00:07:32.711
I thought I would see it, you know,

00:07:32.711 --> 00:07:34.743
from like a historical perspective.

00:07:34.743 --> 00:07:37.262
I didn't expect it to
affect me like it did.

00:07:37.262 --> 00:07:38.521
- When you learn something about a tragedy

00:07:38.521 --> 00:07:39.827
the next step is sort of like

00:07:39.827 --> 00:07:41.416
oh, what work can be done in order

00:07:41.416 --> 00:07:43.608
to apply the lessons from camp?

00:07:43.608 --> 00:07:44.629
- And there's like this real danger

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:47.462
that this same rhetoric
is going to get out there

00:07:47.462 --> 00:07:48.825
and that people are going to be

00:07:48.825 --> 00:07:52.543
arbitrarily sent off for no reason.

00:07:52.543 --> 00:07:55.246
- Just imagine someone just telling you

00:07:55.246 --> 00:07:57.179
to pack your bag and leave right now.

00:07:57.179 --> 00:07:58.780
- And it's nobody's duty
to read every textbook

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.383
and take every Asian
American studies course

00:08:00.383 --> 00:08:01.909
and learn everything, just showing up

00:08:01.909 --> 00:08:03.416
it's not like saving the world,

00:08:03.416 --> 00:08:05.521
but I think that it's, by knowing more

00:08:05.521 --> 00:08:07.517
and by learning more
and by gaining knowledge

00:08:07.517 --> 00:08:09.372
and building a perspective,
we're ultimately

00:08:09.372 --> 00:08:11.704
contributing to a larger consciousness.

00:08:11.704 --> 00:08:14.954
(gentle guitar music)

